A||||4||mbramble||Engine Blower Fan Problem||||5||
Z||000000||mbramble||10-21-2001||01:16 PM||||I posted about this once before but nothing was ever figured out about it.  <P>My radiator fan will not come on if I have the relay for the engine blower connected.  If I have it disconnected and turn on the A/C so that the rad fan comes on, and then I plug in the eng fan relay, the front fan turns off.  <P>I have cut the 12V wire that feeds the eng fan relay and run 12V to it from the trunk light assembly instead.  Everything works perfectly this way.  So, it's not a bad relay, or a bad fan motor, it appears to be some sort of wiring problem -- possibly at the C500 connector??  I just can't figure it out!!<P>I want to clean out the C500 connector, it is pretty nasty but what type of stuff should I use to put back to ensure waterproff connections??<P>Any ideas with were this problem might be or even how/where to go about checking would be most appreciated.  I have been all over the place with a multi-meter and the only thing that I can find is that if the eng fan is connected up and you turn on the A/C to activate the fans, and neither one will come on -- if you check the 12V at the eng fan relay connector it is down to about 4-5V.  If you disconnect/reconnect C500, the voltage goes back to 12V until you plug the eng fan relay connector back in and activate the fans (by turning on the A/C).||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||10-21-2001||01:50 PM||||I can't begine to guess what's causing the fan relay problem at the moment...<P>As for C500, you can clean it out with regular contact cleaner then repack it with standard dielectric compound once it's dry of cleaner.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||10-21-2001||05:00 PM||||Well, I cleaned both sides of C500 but no changes at all.  According to my schematics the 10A C/H fuse is used only for the two blowers.  Well, I replaced it with a 15A.  Everything works beautifully now..  I'm thinking that maybe one of the two motors is drawing more current than it should, especially at startup and is causing the problem.  I'm just gonna leave the 15A fuse in it and see what happens....||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-21-2001||05:12 PM||||The main cooling fan draws power from a fuse linked feed.<P>In the OE setup, the FAN E fuse powers the fan control line and the aux fan for the alt/coil.<P>It very well could be a startup current problem. keep an eye on it that nothing over heats. A 5 amp fuse change shouldn't be a problem but watch it all the same. If you can get something betwen 10 and 15, I'd even try tweeking it down to that.<P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mbramble||10-21-2001||05:54 PM||||I knew dat!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Guess I wasn't thinking when I was writing.  The front fan is actually powered by a fusible link.  So, it would only be the back fan pulling too much starting current.  I have another eng compartment fan; maybe I will clean it up and see if it works better.<P>Anyway, I'm just glad that it's working.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks for the info..<BR>||||reg||1
